  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM EDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nAn upper speed max is progressing across the Lower Ohio Valley \nand eventually the Mid Atlantic region into this evening. A \nmid/upper level ridge building into the region behind this \nfeature will allow low level cold air advection to continue \nacross the area tonight. The combination of mostly clear skies \nand residual near sfc moisture could allow for another morning \nof patchy shallow fog early Thursday AM, but currently not \nexpecting this to be a major concern. Good radiational cooling \nconditions will result in below normal low temps tonight from \nmid 50s to lower 60s.\n\nThe mid/upper level wave pattern will become somewhat stagnant \ntomorrow and Thursday with little movement in mid/upper level ridge \naxis across the region and persistence of a low level anticyclone. \nThis will result in light northwest winds for Thursday, eventually \nbecome light southwest on Friday as the ridge axis begins to shift \noff to the east.\n\nWater vapor imagery this afternoon depicts next notable upper \nlevel short wave shifting across central CA/NV this afternoon. \nThis feature will slowly ride the longwave ridge across western \nCONUS. A Plains low level jet ahead of this system will result \nin strong low moisture convergence across the northern Plains \nlate Thursday. Guidance continues to indicate enhancement of \nthis ridge topping short wave from convection, with a large part\nof negative low level height tendencies and eastward \nprogression of associated low level cyclonic flow tied to this \ninduced low/mid level diabatic PV anomaly. Some southeast flow \non eastern periphery of this low level circulation may slow \ntransport of best low level moisture axis into the area on \nFriday, with best chances of showers/isolated storms on Friday \nafternoon south of US Route 30 across northwest into portions of\nnorth central Indiana.\n\nBetter elevated moisture advection with this feature still looks to \nwork into the area Friday night into Saturday morning. Low level \nreflection of this system still looks likely to track south of the \nlocal area which may set up a period of deformation forcing into \nSaturday afternoon/early evening. Slow moving nature of this forcing \ncould allow for some localized decent rainfall amounts, but trend in \nmedium range guidance has been to slightly more progression which \nshould prevent significant hydro issues. Severe weather is\ncurrently not expected as better axis of weak/moderate\ninstability appears to be favored well south of the local area.\n\nPassage of Friday/Saturday system will leave a broad low level \nthermal trough in its wake and slightly below normal temperatures \nfor the remainder of the weekend into Monday. Mid/upper level flow \nwill gradually deamplify next week which will eventually allow \neastward migration of warmer central CONUS low level temps. This \ndeamplified pattern will also result in some increased chances of \nshowers/storms toward the end of the period, although confidence in \ntiming of increased chances is low at this time.",
